  "answer_text": "**Medi-Cal** is California\u2019s Medicaid program. It provides free or low-cost health, dental, and vision coverage to eligible California residents. \n\n---\n\n### 1. What Does Medi-Cal Cover?\nMedi-Cal offers comprehensive, full-scope medical benefits. Some of the primary services covered include:\n*   **Medical Care:** Doctor visits, preventative care, annual checkups, immunizations, and specialist care.\n*   **Emergency Services:** Hospital stays, emergency room visits, and urgent care.\n*   **Prescriptions:** Outpatient prescription medications.\n*   **Mental Health & Substance Use:** Counseling, therapy, and substance abuse treatment.\n*   **Dental Care:** Known as *Denti-Cal*, it covers cleanings, x-rays, fillings, and other dental procedures.\n*   **Vision Care:** Routine eye exams and glasses (for certain qualified members).\n*   **Long-Term Care:** Services at skilled nursing or intermediate care facilities, and non-emergency medical transportation.\n\n---\n\n### 2. General Eligibility & Income Limits\nEligibility is primarily determined by your household size and income. The current income thresholds are tied to the Federal Poverty Level (FPL):\n*   **Adults (ages 19\u201364):** Generally eligible if household income is at or below **138% of the FPL**.\n*   **Children (under age 19):** Generally eligible if household income is at or below **266% of the FPL**.\n*   **Other Pathways:** Pregnant individuals, people aged 65 or older, individuals with disabilities, and those in foster care may also qualify under different income guidelines or categories.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Changes (What's New in 2026 & Beyond)\nCalifornia has implemented significant structural updates to the Medi-Cal program:\n\n*   **Asset Limit Reinstatement (Effective January 1, 2026):** \n    After completely eliminating asset limits in 2024, California has reinstated asset checks for \"Classic\" Medi-Cal (older adults, people with disabilities, and individuals needing long-term nursing care). \n    *   **The Individual Asset Limit:** **$130,000** for a single person, plus **$65,000** for each additional family member (up to 10 people).\n    *   *What counts:* Bank accounts, cash, and additional properties/vehicles beyond your primary residence.\n    *   *What does not count:* Your primary home (where you live), one vehicle, and personal household items.\n*   **Immigration Coverage Changes (Effective January 1, 2026):**\n    While California previously expanded full-scope Medi-Cal to all low-income residents regardless of immigration status, an enrollment freeze began on January 1, 2026. \n    *   **Existing Members:** If you already had full-scope Medi-Cal before 2026, you will keep your full coverage as long as you renew on time. \n    *   **New Applicants:** New adult applicants without satisfactory immigration status will be enrolled in *restricted-scope* Medi-Cal, which covers emergency and pregnancy services.\n*   **Upcoming Work Requirements (Effective January 1, 2027):** \n    Under federal updates (H.R. 1), starting in 2027, certain working-age adults (19\u201364) on Medi-Cal will need to meet work, school, or community service requirements to keep coverage unless they qualify for an exemption (such as having a disability or caregiving duties).\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Apply\nYou can apply for Medi-Cal at any time throughout the year:\n1.  **Online (Recommended):** Apply through **[BenefitsCal](https://benefitscal.com/)** or **[Covered California](https://www.coveredca.com/)**. Both portals share an application that automatically determines if you qualify for Medi-Cal or subsidized private insurance.\n2.  **By Phone:** Call your local county social services office or Covered California at (800) 300-1506.\n3.  **In Person / Mail:** You can visit your local county social services agency to fill out a paper application. \n\n### 5. Keeping Your Coverage (Renewals)\nOnce enrolled, you must **renew your coverage annually**. \n*   Always keep your mailing address, phone number, and email updated with your local county office so you do not miss your renewal packet. \n*   If you receive a renewal packet in the mail, you must fill it out and submit it by the deadline to prevent a lapse in your health insurance.",
